<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malaita</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malaita is the most populous island of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-islands">Solomon Islands</a>, a long mountainous island north-east of Guadalcanal. From the late 19th century many Malaitans were recruited as labourers for Queensland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> and later for plantations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomons</a>. The island resisted colonial control, notably in the killing of district officer William Bell in 1927 and the Maasina Rule movement after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malaita.jpg" alt="Malaita" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malaita_Island_NASA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Malaita is a thin island about 164 kilometres long and 37 kilometres wide at its widest point. To the south-west the Indispensable Strait separates it from Guadalcanal, and to the north-west lies Santa Isabel. A central ridge runs along its length, rising to about 980 metres in the north and 1,300 metres in the south, and the coastal plain is very narrow. The people of the Langa Langa and Lau lagoons call themselves wane i asi, salt-water people, to distinguish themselves from the wane i tolo, bush people, of the interior. The main languages include To'abaita, Kwara'ae, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwaio">Kwaio</a> and ꞋAreꞌare, and the traditional religion is ancestor worship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first recorded European sighting was by the Spaniard Álvaro de Mendaña on 11 April 1568, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-islands">Solomon Islands</a> were then not found again for two hundred years. From the 1870s to 1903 Malaitan men, and some women, formed the largest number of Solomon Islander workers in the indentured labour trade to Queensland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, which began with illegal recruiting known as blackbirding. Returning labourers had often learnt to read and become Christian, and the Queensland Kanaka Mission sent its first party to the Solomons in 1904.</p><p>There was no central power on the island, and blood feuds were made worse by guns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> proclaimed the British Solomon Islands Protectorate in 1893, and Auki became a government station in 1909. District Commissioner William R. Bell was killed by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwaio">Kwaio</a> in 1927, with a cadet and 13 Solomon Islanders in his charge. A punitive expedition followed, known as the Malaita massacre, in which at least 60 Kwaio were killed, nearly 200 detained at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulagi">Tulagi</a> and many sacred sites destroyed or desecrated.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, Malaitans who had served in battalions started Maasina Ruru, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maasina-rule">Marching Rule</a>, a movement for self-determination that united people across religious, ethnic and clan lines and refused to cooperate with the British. In September 1947 most of its chiefs were tried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honiara">Honiara</a> and convicted. The creation of the Malaita Council, the first local government in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-islands">Solomon Islands</a>, eased tensions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Malaita had a population of 172,740 in 2019. Its largest city and provincial capital is Auki, on the northwest coast. Most Malaitans live by subsistence farming, with taro and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweet-potato">sweet potatoes</a> the main crops, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shell-money">shell money</a> made in the Langa Langa lagoon remains a traditional currency.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malaita” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Resistance continued, notably on <b>Malaita</b>, where Kwaio warriors killed the district officer William Bell in 1927.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-solomon-islands-protectorate">British Solomon Islands Protectorate</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1998 the Isatabu Freedom Movement on Guadalcanal and the <b>Malaita</b> Eagle Force fought, and about 200 people were killed; the economy collapsed and the government went bankrupt.</blockquote><a href="/state/solomon-islands">Solomon Islands</a></li></ul></section>
